{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:historian","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:fcf47bd3d55e6ceed5482283df4883663f8b84bc6826c7ead33d23818de261d8","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.17,"B":0.45,"C":0.27,"D":0.11}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"HISTORICAL BAND FREQUENCIES FIRST (the brief's mandatory reference class). Taking 5-year windows of OECD+China PPP real growth since 1960 and sorting by the CQ3 growth measure alone: pre-1990 windows land almost entirely in the >= 2.6% band (A's growth gate) — roughly 70-75% of 1960-1989 windows; 1990-2010 windows ~60% in the >=2.6% band; 2011-2025 windows ~55% at or above 2.6% (dropping below only in 2019-2023 and 2020-2024). So the UNCONDITIONAL band frequency would put most mass on A. I reject that as the answer and keep A at 17% because the decline is mechanical, not stochastic: the locked 2A arithmetic (OECD per-capita 1.0->0.6, China -2.7pp, China's weight ~33%) removes the growth that put history in that band. This is exactly the \"technology will offset demography\" error the lens is built to resist, so I note the tension rather than inherit the frequency. CONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ3. My CQ3 is P10 0.8 / P50 1.9 / P90 2.9, implying σ ≈ 0.82. Under the lexical lock: (1) A = P(growth >= 2.6%) = P(z >= 0.85) ≈ 0.20; I shade to 0.17 for the downward trend and for the right-tail thinness the PPP weights impose. (2) P(growth < 2.0%) = P(z < 0.12) ≈ 0.55. (3) P(growth < 1.7%) = P(z < -0.24) ≈ 0.40. CONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ2. My CQ2 conjunct A put P(OECD-38 GDP-weighted core >= 2.50% over 2027-2036) = 70%, driven largely by the mechanical EM-member uplift in the OECD-38 GDP-weighted aggregate (Turkey/Mexico/Colombia/Chile). For the tighter 2032-2036-only window at the higher >= 2.70% gate, mean reversion pulls down but fiscal dominance pushes up: P ≈ 0.45. Symmetrically, P(core < 2.00% over 2032-2036) ≈ 0.20 — LOW, and this is the single most under-appreciated arithmetic fact in the CQ6 lock, because it structurally starves regime D. C = P(core >= 2.7%) × P(growth < 2.0% | high core) = 0.45 × 0.55 × 1.15 (mild positive stagflationary dependence, the 1970s bottleneck template) ≈ 0.285 -> 27. D = P(growth < 1.7%) × P(core < 2.0% | growth < 1.7%) = 0.40 × 0.28 ≈ 0.11 -> 11 (the conditional is lifted above the 0.20 unconditional because a demand shortfall is itself disinflationary — the Japan 1998-2012 template — but capped by the OECD-38 composition effect). B = residual = 100 - 17 - 27 - 11 = 45. INTERNAL AUDIT: B must contain (i) the 1.7-2.5% growth band and (ii) the low-growth/moderate-inflation leftover cell. My implied leftover = P(growth<2.0%) - C - D = 0.55 - 0.27 - 0.11 = 0.17, which sits inside B; the remaining 0.28 of B is the 2.0-2.5% growth band. Both are internally consistent with CQ3's σ and with CQ2's inflation marginals. I do not put most mass on A despite my CQ3 P50 of 1.9%, as the lock requires.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:58.874Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD harmonised unemployment rate (monthly OECD Labour Statistics release) prints >= 6.0% for three consecutive months.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 collapses from 13 to ~5 (conjunct 1 becomes near-impossible to average back under 5.5% across 2032-2036 from a recessionary start). CQ3 P50 falls ~0.2pp to ~1.7%; CQ6-D rises ~6pp to ~17% and CQ6-A falls ~4pp. Asset matrix: raises G7 long nominal bonds and lowers construction/trades and industrial metals in the Buildout sub-period.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) 12-month trailing averag